#!/bin/rc
# archive-roundtrip -- does xz/lz4 survive a LOCAL round trip at size?
#
#	./archive-roundtrip [samplefile ...]
#
# No arguments needed: it builds its own corpus and looks for the two
# shipped samples in `.', `/tmp' and `$home/download'.  Any paths given
# are tried as samples too, so a file somewhere else needs no editing.
#
# ---- WHAT IT COMES FROM --------------------------------------------
#
# Two decode failures are recorded as OPEN and UNSEPARATED:
#
#	unxz xz_sample_file_1MB.xz   -> Compressed data is corrupt
#	unlz4 sample.txt.lz4         -> Error 26 : LZ4F frame decoding
#	                                could not complete: invalid frame
#
# and the reason they are unseparated is that **both files were
# produced ELSEWHERE**, so a bad download and a real decode bug look
# exactly alike.
#
# ***ONE OF THOSE TWO EXPLANATIONS IS NOW DEAD.***  `unrar x' on a
# downloaded archive answers `All OK', and unrar verifies a CRC32 over
# every header and over the extracted data -- which is a checksum over
# the whole download path.  *So `hget' is not corrupting files*, and
# what is left is a port bug or a genuinely bad source file.
#
# **A LOCAL round trip is what separates THOSE two**, because it never
# touches the network: this tree compresses its own bytes and reads
# them back.
#
# ---- THE THREE-WAY READING, WRITTEN DOWN BEFORE THE RUN ------------
#
#   -T1 passes, -T0 FAILS
#	The THREADED encoder.  That is ground nothing has covered --
#	`pthread_create' was broken until this release and every xz
#	run before it either hung or never reached a worker, so a fix
#	that lets a process reach code it never reached before can
#	expose anything on that path.
#   BOTH pass
#	The port is fine at size and the shipped sample files are the
#	problem.  *Then the open item closes as "not ours".*
#   BOTH fail
#	The codec is broken at size, and the small file passing was
#	luck -- `xz -c <sample.txt' is 72 bytes, one block, one
#	thread, no multi-block framing at all.
#   bytes MATCH and the tool reports an ERROR
#	The FRAME and not the payload.  **This is lz4's answer and v1
#	could not say it** -- see the three faults below.
#
# ---- v1 RAN AND HAD THREE FAULTS, ALL MINE -------------------------
#
# The first run printed, in two adjacent lines:
#
#	Error 26 : LZ4F frame decoding could not complete: invalid frame
#	PASS: lz4 round trip
#
# **A contradiction the script reported as a pass**, because `check'
# compared the BYTES and ignored every tool's exit STATUS.  *This
# tree's own "a check that can pass for the wrong reason", and the run
# printed the evidence against itself.*
#
# What it means is worth more than the fault: the payload came back
# byte-identical, so **lz4 round-trips the DATA and the decoder
# rejects the FRAME** -- an end mark or a content checksum, not the
# compressed stream.  And the file was made HERE, so the shipped
# sample's identical message is not a download at all.
#
#   1. **Status ignored.**  Every compress and decompress is checked
#      now, SEPARATELY from the byte comparison, because `the bytes
#      match but the tool said no' is a third outcome and the most
#      informative one.
#
#   2. **The corpus was short, the reason was hidden, and my first
#      reading of WHY was wrong.**  `cat' had its stderr sent to
#      `/dev/null', so a failure would have been invisible -- *a
#      redirection added for tidiness deleting the only evidence* --
#      and the VM's 342880 bytes against the host's 1053 files and
#      1.8MB for the same glob read as exactly that.
#      ***IT IS NOT.***  **Nothing binds this repository's `sys/src'
#      over `/sys/src'** -- `mount-include' binds HEADERS and
#      LIBRARIES and no sources at all -- so `/sys/src/ape/lib/ap' on
#      the VM is **9front's own stock APE libap**, a different and
#      much smaller tree than the checkout the host number came from.
#      *Two trees with one path, compared as though they were one.*
#      The repair is kept because it is right for its own reasons: it
#      appends per directory, leaves stderr alone, and prints the
#      count -- so a corpus that really is short says so instead of
#      being inferred.  **And the corpus takes more than one source
#      now**, since 342KB was thin for the question whatever its
#      cause.
#
#   3. ***SECTION 2 COULD NOT FAIL.***  xz's threaded encoder splits
#      the input into BLOCKS and gives one block to each worker, and
#      the default block size is about three times the dictionary --
#      ~24MB at `-6'.  A 342KB input is therefore ONE block and ONE
#      worker however many `-T0' asks for, so `-T0 passed' said
#      nothing about the threaded path, which is the only thing that
#      section exists to ask about.  `--block-size=65536' is what
#      makes it able to fail.
#
# ---- HOW IT IS BUILT, AND WHY IN THIS ORDER ------------------------
#
# **Each case announces itself BEFORE it runs**, because a fault, a
# kill and a clean finish all just give the prompt back, and the
# threaded arm is the one with a recorded history of HANGING.  The
# `-T1' case therefore runs first: *every case expected to return
# comes before every case expected to hang*, so a freeze names itself
# by what printed last.  **The completion marker at the end is what
# says the run reached it** -- read that before reading any count.
#
# **The corpus is this tree's own `lib/ap' sources**, varied C rather
# than a repeated file on purpose, since eight copies of one file
# compress to almost nothing and would exercise neither multi-block
# framing nor the worker split, which is the whole question.
#
# **`cmp' is read by its STATUS and not by its silence.**  APE's `cmp'
# prints nothing when two files are the same, and it also printed
# nothing for years when it wrongly believed its own stdout was
# `/dev/null' -- *indistinguishable from the prompt*.  The status is
# correct either way.
#
# **The pipe and the file cases are separate for lz4**, and that is
# not a tidier spelling of one case: a frame that is whole file-to-file
# and short through a pipe is a stdio FINALISATION bug in libap, and
# one that is short both ways is lz4's own frame writer.  Different
# repairs, so they are asked separately.
#
# ***AND THERE IS NO GENERIC `run this and check its status' HELPER,
# WHICH IS DELIBERATE.***  In rc the redirections on
# `step label xz -c <in >out' apply to **`step'**, so the function's
# own `echo' would land in the output FILE.  `$status' is read on the
# line after each command instead; *a helper that corrupts the data it
# is checking is worse than no helper.*

# **The failure count is a LIST and not arithmetic.**  rc has no
# integer arithmetic, so a counter means shelling out to `dc' or `awk'
# for every case; `$#list' is the language's own answer and cannot
# mis-parse.
failed = ()
tmp = /tmp/archive-roundtrip.$pid
big = $tmp/big

fn note {
	echo $*
}

fn fail {
	note '  FAIL: '^$1
	failed = ($failed $1)
}

# $1 the label, $2 the `$status' of the command on the line above.
fn tookay {
	if(~ $2 '')
		note '    ok: '^$1
	if not
		fail $1^' -- THE TOOL REPORTED AN ERROR (status '^$2^')'
}

fn cmpfiles {
	# $1 the label, $2 and $3 the two files
	cmp $2 $3 >[2]/dev/null
	if(~ $status '')
		note '  PASS: '^$1^' -- bytes identical'
	if not
		fail $1^' -- BYTES DIFFER'
}

note 'archive-roundtrip: starting'

# ---- gate --------------------------------------------------------
# A missing tool and a failing tool are different results, and a run
# that silently skips the case it was built for is the shape this
# tree's notes call a check that cannot fail.
havexz = yes
havelz4 = yes
if(! test -x /bin/xz && ! test -x /bin/ape/xz){
	note 'archive-roundtrip: no xz in the path -- its sections are SKIPPED,'
	note 'archive-roundtrip: which is not the same as passing.'
	havexz = no
}
if(! test -x /bin/lz4 && ! test -x /bin/ape/lz4){
	note 'archive-roundtrip: no lz4 in the path -- its sections are SKIPPED,'
	note 'archive-roundtrip: which is not the same as passing.'
	havelz4 = no
}

mkdir -p $tmp
# **Several sources, because ONE of them is smaller than it looks.**
# `/sys/src/ape/lib/ap' on the VM is stock 9front's APE libap and not
# this repository -- nothing binds the repo's sources anywhere -- so
# it is about 340KB rather than the 1.8MB the checkout holds.  The
# extra roots are there to get the corpus into the megabytes, and each
# is reported separately so a missing one is visible rather than
# inferred.
>$big
nfiles = ()
for(d in /sys/src/ape/lib/ap/* /sys/src/libc/port /sys/src/cmd /sys/src/cmd/aux /sys/src/cmd/ip){
	if(test -d $d){
		n = ()
		for(f in $d/*.c){
			if(test -f $f){
				cat $f >>$big
				n = ($n x)
				nfiles = ($nfiles x)
			}
		}
		if(! ~ $#n 0)
			note '  '^$#n^' files from '^$d
	}
}
if(! test -s $big){
	note 'archive-roundtrip: corpus is empty -- no C sources found under'
	note 'archive-roundtrip: /sys/src.  Nothing below can run.'
	exit nocorpus
}
# `wc -c' and NOT `ls -l | awk {print $6}'.  Plan 9's `ls -l' puts the
# size in field 6 and GNU coreutils' puts it in field 5 -- and inside
# `apexp-sh' the union resolves `ls' to **GNU's**, which is this tree's
# own recorded trap (`basename -d' answering `invalid option').  `wc -c'
# has one field and no opinion.
note 'corpus: '^`{wc -c <$big}^' bytes from '^$#nfiles^' files'
note 'corpus: under ~400KB means only stock APE''s libap was found, which'
note 'corpus: still gives several blocks at --block-size=65536.'

# ---- section 1: xz -T1, the single-threaded encoder ---------------
if(~ $havexz yes){
	note 'section 1: xz -T1 (single thread) round trip'
	note '  compressing'
	xz -T1 -c <$big >$tmp/t1.xz
	tookay 'xz -T1 compress' $"status
	note '  decompressing'
	unxz -c <$tmp/t1.xz >$tmp/t1.out
	tookay 'unxz -T1 output' $"status
	cmpfiles 'xz -T1 round trip' $big $tmp/t1.out
}

# ---- section 2: xz -T0, the threaded encoder, REALLY threaded -----
# ***THIS IS THE ONE THAT CAN HANG***, which is why it is second and
# why the line before it prints first.  `--block-size' is what makes
# it able to fail at all; see fault 3 in the header.
if(~ $havexz yes){
	note 'section 2: xz -T0 --block-size=65536 (really threaded)'
	note '  compressing -- THIS IS THE ARM WITH A HISTORY OF HANGING'
	xz -T0 --block-size=65536 -c <$big >$tmp/t0.xz
	tookay 'xz -T0 compress' $"status
	note '  decompressing'
	unxz -c <$tmp/t0.xz >$tmp/t0.out
	tookay 'unxz -T0 output' $"status
	cmpfiles 'xz -T0 blocked round trip' $big $tmp/t0.out
}

# ---- section 3: lz4 through a pipe, THREADED compressor -----------
# ***THE THREADPOOL IS ON IN THIS PORT AND UPSTREAM LEAVES IT OFF.***
# `cmd/lz4/mkfile' passes `-DLZ4IO_MULTITHREAD', while
# `programs/lz4conf.h:45' defaults it to **0** on every non-Windows
# target with the comment "Requires <pthread> support.  Can't be
# reliably and portably tested at source code level" -- so this port
# runs lz4's threaded paths on the pthreads THIS RELEASE fixed, which
# is ground nothing has covered.
#
# **The host clears upstream's logic**: the same sources built with
# gcc and `-DLZ4IO_MULTITHREAD=1' round-trip 1804488 bytes both
# through a pipe and file-to-file, status 0 each way.  *So the
# question is this platform's and not lz4's.*
if(~ $havelz4 yes){
	note 'section 3: lz4 through a PIPE, default (threaded) compressor'
	note '  compressing'
	lz4 -c <$big >$tmp/p.lz4
	tookay 'lz4 compress (pipe, threaded)' $"status
	note '  decompressing'
	unlz4 -c <$tmp/p.lz4 >$tmp/p.out
	tookay 'unlz4 decompress (pipe)' $"status
	cmpfiles 'lz4 pipe round trip' $big $tmp/p.out
}

# ---- section 4: lz4 -T1, the UNthreaded compressor ----------------
# ***`-T1' IS COMPRESSION ONLY AND THAT IS THE POINT.***  The decode
# path calls `TPool_create(1, 1)' unconditionally
# (`lz4io.c:2113'), so **decompression is threaded whatever `-T'
# says** -- which makes this a one-sided switch and therefore a
# discriminator:
#
#   -T1 and the default produce the SAME bytes
#	The compressor is cleared, and the error belongs to the
#	DECODER's threadpool.  `lastStatus' is a parent STACK
#	variable written by the worker through a pointer
#	(`lz4io.c:2074') and read after `TPool_jobsCompleted'
#	(`:2178'), so a job that has not run, or a write the parent
#	does not see, reads the initial 1 -- *and nonzero there means
#	exactly "the decoder still wants more input", which with
#	complete output is a missing END MARK and not bad data.*
#   they DIFFER
#	The threaded compressor is writing a different frame, and
#	the worker split is the suspect.
if(~ $havelz4 yes){
	note 'section 4: lz4 -T1 (single-threaded compressor) through a pipe'
	note '  compressing'
	lz4 -T1 -c <$big >$tmp/s.lz4
	tookay 'lz4 compress (pipe, -T1)' $"status
	note '  decompressing'
	unlz4 -c <$tmp/s.lz4 >$tmp/s.out
	tookay 'unlz4 decompress (-T1 stream)' $"status
	cmpfiles 'lz4 -T1 pipe round trip' $big $tmp/s.out
	# Reported, not counted: this is the discriminator above and it
	# is a question, not a rule -- two compressors may legitimately
	# differ.  *What it is READ for is which side to look at next.*
	cmp $tmp/p.lz4 $tmp/s.lz4 >[2]/dev/null
	if(~ $status '')
		note '  NOTE: threaded and -T1 streams are IDENTICAL -- the'
		note '  NOTE: compressor is cleared, look at the DECODER pool.'
	if not
		note '  NOTE: threaded and -T1 streams DIFFER -- look at the'
		note '  NOTE: threaded COMPRESSOR and its worker split.'
}

# ---- section 5: lz4 file to file ----------------------------------
# **A separate case and not a tidier spelling of section 3.**  With
# `-c' the destination is stdout, which `lz4io.c:1464' deliberately
# does NOT `fclose' -- so a frame that is whole file-to-file and short
# through a pipe would be a stdio finalisation bug, and one that is
# short both ways is lz4's own frame writer.  Different repairs.
# (libap's `exit' does call `__stdio_exit', which flushes stdout, so
# that reading is already weak -- *the case is here to make it
# measured rather than argued.*)
if(~ $havelz4 yes){
	note 'section 5: lz4 FILE to FILE'
	note '  compressing'
	lz4 -f $big $tmp/f.lz4
	tookay 'lz4 compress (file)' $"status
	note '  decompressing'
	unlz4 -f $tmp/f.lz4 $tmp/f.out
	tookay 'unlz4 decompress (file)' $"status
	cmpfiles 'lz4 file round trip' $big $tmp/f.out
}

# ---- section 6: the tail of each lz4 stream -----------------------
# **Reported and NOT counted**, and there is a REFERENCE for it: an
# lz4 frame ends with the EndMark, four zero bytes, and then the
# content checksum, which the CLI writes by default.  The host build
# of these same sources ends
#
#	... 00 00 00 00 ea 08 55 0a
#	    `--- EndMark   `--- content checksum
#
# so **eight bytes, the first four zero**.  A stream here that ends
# on compressed data instead has lost its mark and the WRITER is at
# fault; one that ends correctly convicts the reader.  *This is the
# one observation that separates those without a rebuild.*
if(~ $havelz4 yes){
	note 'section 6: the tail of each lz4 stream (reported, not counted)'
	note '  want: 4 zero bytes (EndMark) then 4 checksum bytes.'
	note '  the host reference ends 00 00 00 00 ea 08 55 0a'
	for(f in $tmp/p.lz4 $tmp/s.lz4 $tmp/f.lz4){
		if(test -f $f){
			note '  '^$f^', '^`{wc -c <$f}^' bytes, last 16:'
			xd -1x $f | tail -2
		}
	}
}

# ---- section 7: the shipped samples, if they are here -------------
# **Reported and NOT counted.**  These are the files the open item is
# about, and whether they decode is the question the local round trip
# exists to put a background under -- it is not an assertion about
# this tree.  *A sample that fails while the round trip passes says
# the file is bad; one that fails while the round trip also fails says
# nothing by itself.*
note 'section 7: the shipped sample files (reported, not counted)'
# `$home' is guarded and concatenated with `^' EXPLICITLY.  An unset
# variable inside a concatenation is a null LIST in rc, which is an
# error and not an empty string -- `null list in concatenation', the
# same shape as `mkone''s unset `BIN' and as `eccp''s `$O'.
xzsamples = (./xz_sample_file_1MB.xz /tmp/xz_sample_file_1MB.xz $*)
lz4samples = (./sample.txt.lz4 /tmp/sample.txt.lz4 $*)
if(! ~ $#home 0){
	xzsamples = ($xzsamples $home^/download/xz_sample_file_1MB.xz)
	lz4samples = ($lz4samples $home^/download/sample.txt.lz4)
}
for(f in $xzsamples){
	if(test -f $f){
		note '  trying '^$f
		unxz -c <$f >/dev/null
		if(~ $status '')
			note '  sample xz: decodes'
		if not
			note '  sample xz: FAILS'
	}
}
for(f in $lz4samples){
	if(test -f $f){
		note '  trying '^$f
		unlz4 -c <$f >/dev/null
		if(~ $status '')
			note '  sample lz4: decodes'
		if not
			note '  sample lz4: FAILS'
	}
}

rm -rf $tmp
note ''
note 'archive-roundtrip: COMPLETE, '^$#failed^' failure(s)'
if(! ~ $#failed 0)
	note 'archive-roundtrip: failed: '^$"failed
note 'archive-roundtrip: -T1 pass + -T0 fail = the threaded encoder;'
note 'archive-roundtrip: both pass = the shipped samples are the problem;'
note 'archive-roundtrip: both fail = the codec is broken at size;'
note 'archive-roundtrip: bytes identical + tool error = the FRAME, not the data.'
exit $#failed
